linc (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A modelled cycle time: the bar's remainder is planned from the program's
    own words - each block's feed, arcs by sweep, rapids at the INI's
    MAX_VELOCITY, dwells as holds - with the overrides applied where the figure
    is read, so a knob turned mid-run moves it on that frame. Each finished
    stretch is measured against the plan and kept per move in
    <profile>.cycletime.toml, so the next run of the same program starts
    calibrated. The clock reads elapsed over the estimate, `CYCLE 0:01:12 /
    ~0:04:52`.
  * A program has to pass a syntax check before the interpreter is given it,
    in three layers: a block-by-block lint that reports every fault at once,
    the preview parser's single fault, and the controller's own `rs274 -g`
    against the machine INI. A refused file still opens in the editor with
    each fault marked and its full message in a box under the line, walked
    with Ctrl+PageUp/Ctrl+PageDown. A toolpath that leaves the machine's
    travel box is a fault on the same list, and `linc.program-ok` closes the
    HAL side of the gate.
  * Cycle start is the HMI's command now: checked against the machine and the
    program, sent as EMC_TASK_PLAN_RUN or as EMC_TASK_PLAN_RESUME when the
    program is held, and refused with the one thing to fix. The panel holds
    cnc_panel.cycle_start for 200 ms, so a tap shorter than the 20 Hz snapshot
    still arrives. A program that dies mid-run holds the machine instead of
    running out the moves the planner already held.
  * A coordinate matrix as MAIN's second face, in AUTO, MDI and the manual
    screens: WORK COORDINATES under the active WCS, MACHINE COORDINATES,
    DISTANCE TO GO and TOOL OFFSETS. The active WCS edits in place - click a
    row or its letter, type an expression, Enter writes it back - the toolpath
    keeps a column beside it, and the chosen face survives a restart.
  * Tool offsets in X and Y, not only Z, from the offset words the INI's tool
    table declares. Work offsets are written behind G90 and the machine's own
    units, so a keypad value means what the screen says whatever the
    interpreter was left in.
  * Machine fixes: `E-STOP ?` only on a chain that loses its power with the
    cabinet, the session kept through the machine-on drop E-stop causes, the
    limits override armed from MANUAL where task accepts it, every interlock
    gate added to a thread, flood and lube gated on machine-on, and a panel
    link that resyncs instead of closing on a late reply.
  * Debian packages built for bookworm by default, from the tree on disk
    rather than cargo's mtimes, reusing an unchanged build image.

 -- Hans Baier <foss@hans-baier.de>  Tue, 24 Nov 2026 12:40:00 +0700

linc (0.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A profile logo in the top bar: [ui] logo draws a PNG or GIF in place of
    the LiNC wordmark, scaled to the bar, with the machine name kept beside
    it. A missing file fails --check-config; an image that cannot be decoded
    keeps the wordmark and logs one warning.
  * Spindle-speed keys on the manual screens: Ctrl+PgUp/PgDn step the running
    spindle's programmed speed by the machine's increment and Ctrl+S enters an
    absolute speed. Both edit the speed, not the override, and a running
    program's next S word still wins.
  * A CUT card for machines that publish no load feedback, so the second
    card on the Machine page carries chip load instead of an empty meter.
  * The shell never scales below the design floor, and the window cannot be
    made smaller than the 1280x720 baseline.
  * Debian packages for bookworm and trixie, built in containers against the
    release's own glibc and LinuxCNC 2.9.

 -- Hans Baier <foss@hans-baier.de>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 08:20:00 +0700

linc (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Sidebar SHORTCUTS rows that name a single command are clickable: the mouse
    runs the same dispatch as the key, with hover feedback and a pointer
    cursor. Gesture rows (PAN, ZOOM, the arrow keys, multi-key filters) stay
    legends. All page-local commands route through the page that owns them.
  * Tool table search, per-axis servo load, spindle inhibit for the whole tool
    change, hard-limit recovery, and the tool in the spindle restored after a
    reboot.
  * Probe category tabs are clickable; probing still runs the vendored Probe
    Basic routines.
  * First release with the operator manual, warranty and safety notice.

 -- Hans Baier <foss@hans-baier.de>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 10:20:00 +0700

linc (0.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Ship the machine-facing half only: the three binaries, the shared
    base.toml defaults the profile extends, the reference HAL snippets and the
    reviewed probe bundle. No simulation profile and no desktop entry - those
    belong to a development checkout.
  * A machine profile lives in the integrator's config directory and points
    its [DISPLAY] entry at linc-display.sh, which loads POSTGUI_HALFILE itself:
    LinuxCNC does not.

